+## Decisions & Alternatives
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| How concurrent independent edits to one visit become a current record | Whole-visit revisions: each edit names a base revision; stale-base edits become competing whole revisions a coordinator selects between | Field-aware three-way merge (auto-combine disjoint field changes, review overlaps) | The server cannot tell a scientifically related pair of edits from an unrelated one by field name. Whole-visit revisions never machine-combine observations, at the cost of review work for disjoint edits. The team accepted a larger review queue over maintaining merge rules that could silently combine related edits. |
+| Who authors a revision ID | The server assigns revision IDs | Client-minted revision IDs | The visit UUID is already client-generated for offline creation; revision IDs are server-side ordering facts and need the server's authority. Base revision IDs that clients send are ones the server previously issued. |
+| What resolution produces | Designation of an existing revision in the competing set as the current revision | Resolution mints a new merged payload on the server | Keeping resolution a selection, not an authored payload, keeps field combination out of the server per the chosen mechanism. A coordinator who wants a hand-combined record submits it as an ordinary revision (then resolves to it); authoring UI belongs to review-export. |
 
